    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold mt-8 mb-8">EU eIDAS Regulation</h1>

    <h2>What is eIDAS?</h2>
    <p>eIDAS (Electronic Identification, Authentication and Trust Services) is an EU regulation that establishes a standard framework for electronic signatures across the European Union, adopted in 2014 and effective since 2016.</p>

    <h2>Types of Electronic Signatures under eIDAS</h2>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Standard Electronic Signature (SES):</strong> Broadly defined as data in electronic form which is attached to or logically associated with other data in electronic form and which is used by the signatory to sign. Suitable for most B2B and B2C transactions.</li>
        <li><strong>Advanced Electronic Signature (AES):</strong> Must be uniquely linked to the signatory, capable of identifying them, created using electronic signature creation data that the signatory can use under their sole control, and linked to the data signed in such a way that any subsequent change in the data is detectable.</li>
        <li><strong>Qualified Electronic Signature (QES):</strong> An AES that is created by a qualified electronic signature creation device, and which is based on a qualified certificate for electronic signatures. It carries the exact same legal effect as a handwritten signature across all EU member states.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How usignpdf complies</h2>
    <p>usignpdf provides Standard Electronic Signatures (SES) which are legally valid for the vast majority of commercial agreements, employment contracts, and NDAs in the EU. Our robust <a href="/security/audit-trails">Audit Trail</a> helps prove intent and document integrity, bolstering the enforceability of the SES.</p>

    <h2>The Burden of Proof (A Worked Example)</h2>
    <p>Under eIDAS Article 25(1), an electronic signature cannot be denied legal effect solely because it is in electronic form. However, the burden of proof differs between the tiers:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>SES / AES Scenario:</strong> If you use usignpdf to sign a <a href="/templates/freelance-contract">B2B contract</a> (SES) and a dispute goes to court in France, the burden is on the person attempting to enforce the signature to prove it is valid. You would introduce the usignpdf <a href="/security/audit-trails">Audit Trail</a> (IP address, email hash, timestamp) as evidence. The judge evaluates this evidence just as they would witness testimony for a wet signature.</li>
        <li><strong>QES Scenario:</strong> A QES reverses the burden of proof. It carries a presumption of legal validity across the EU. If someone challenges a QES, the burden is on them to prove the signature was forged. QES is typically only used for high-value government or real estate transactions due to the high friction of identity verification.</li>
    </ul>

